I now have the complete trace. Here is the full path for POST /api/owners.

## Overview

Active profiles (`src/main/resources/application.properties:20`) are `h2,spring-data-jpa`, so the live repository is the Spring Data JPA one. Security is disabled by default (`petclinic.security.enable=false`, line 49), so the `@PreAuthorize` guard is not enforced unless that property is flipped to `true`.

The route is defined on a generated OpenAPI interface and implemented by the V1 controller.

---

## 1. The @RestController method (POST /api/owners)

The mapping itself lives on the generated interface `OwnersApi`, which the controller implements.

`target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/api/OwnersApi.java`
- Line 44: interface is annotated `@Validated`.
- Lines 52, 79-87: the route and body binding:
```java
String PATH_ADD_OWNER = "/owners";
...
@RequestMapping(
    method = RequestMethod.POST,
    value = OwnersApi.PATH_ADD_OWNER,        // "/owners"
    produces = { "application/json" },
    consumes = { "application/json" }
)
default ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(
    @Parameter(name = "OwnerFieldsDto", description = "The pet owner", required = true) @Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto
) { ... }
```
So `@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto` triggers Bean Validation on the body. The class-level `@RequestMapping("/api")` on the controller (below) combines with `/owners` to give `POST /api/owners`.

The actual implementation: `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`
- Class declaration, lines 51-54:
```java
@RestController
@CrossOrigin(exposedHeaders = "errors, content-type")
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class OwnerRestControllerV1 implements OwnersApi {
```
- Constructor injection of `ClinicService`, `OwnerMapper`, `PetMapper`, `VisitMapper` (lines 56-72).
- The `addOwner` method, lines 99-126:
```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")
@Override
public ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto) {
    HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
    Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);
    Collection<Owner> owners = this.clinicService.findAllOwners();
    owner.setRegistrationDate(BusinessDays.effective(owner));
    if (owners.stream()
        .filter(o -> owner.getRegistrationDate().equals(o.getRegistrationDate())).count() >= 100) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);
    }
    if (Identities.isDuplicate(owners, owner)) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }
    if (CityCodes.countInCity(owners, owner) >= 50) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }
    Households.joinHousehold(owners, owner, ownerFieldsDto.getSharesHousehold())
        .forEach(this.clinicService::saveOwner);
    owner.assignCustomerCode(CityCodes.countInCity(owners, owner));
    owner.setNamesakeCount(Namesakes.count(owners, owner));
    owner.setBulkSignupWarning(BulkSignups.exceededDailyLimit(owners, owner));
    this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
    OwnerDto ownerDto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
    headers.setLocation(UriComponentsBuilder.newInstance()
        .path("/api/owners/{id}").buildAndExpand(owner.getId()).toUri());
    return new ResponseEntity<>(ownerDto, headers, HttpStatus.CREATED);
}
```
Note: the method is NOT annotated `@Transactional` at the controller level (only `deleteOwner` at line 145 is). Each `clinicService.saveOwner(...)` call is its own transaction.

---

## 2. Request DTO — `OwnerFieldsDto`

`target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/dto/OwnerFieldsDto.java`

- Class-level custom validation, line 28:
```java
@org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.validation.PostcodeValidation
public class OwnerFieldsDto {
```
- Fields (lines 31-48): `firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone, email, registrationDate, sharesHousehold, postcode`.
- Field-level Bean Validation (on the getters):
  - `firstName` (line 74): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
  - `lastName` (line 95): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}\\.?$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
  - `address` (line 116): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^.*\\S.*$") @Size(min = 1, max = 255)`
  - `city` (line 137): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^.*\\S.*$") @Size(min = 1, max = 80)`
  - `telephone` (line 158): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "...E.164/AU multi-branch regex...") @Size(min = 1, max = 25)`
  - `email` (line 179): `@Size(max = 255) @jakarta.validation.constraints.Email` (optional)
  - `registrationDate` (line 200): `@Valid` (optional `LocalDate`)
  - `postcode` (line 242): `@Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]{4}$")` (optional)
  - `sharesHousehold`: no constraints (optional `Boolean`).

---

## 3. Custom validators / groups

There is one class-level custom validator applied to the DTO. No Bean Validation `groups` are used.

- Annotation: `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/PostcodeValidation.java` — `@Target(TYPE)`, `@Constraint(validatedBy = PostcodeValidator.class)`, default message "Postcode is not valid for the city's region".
- Validator: `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/PostcodeValidator.java`, lines 17-37. Cross-field check: if postcode is a 4-digit string, it must fall in the range for the city — `Sydney` 2000-2099, `Melbourne` 3000-3099, `Brisbane` 4000-4099 (lines 19-22); unknown cities or non-4-digit values pass.

(There is also an unrelated `PetAgeValidation`/`PetAgeValidator` pair under `rest/validation/`, used by pet endpoints, not by owner creation.)

---

## 4. Service-layer method(s)

The controller calls two `ClinicService` methods during creation: `findAllOwners()` and `saveOwner(...)`.

`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`
- `findAllOwners()`, lines 125-129:
```java
@Override
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public Collection<Owner> findAllOwners() throws DataAccessException {
    return ownerRepository.findAll();
}
```
- `saveOwner(Owner)`, lines 232-237:
```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```
The service is a thin facade; it has no validation, uniqueness, or business logic of its own. All the business rules run in the controller via the helper classes below. `saveOwner` is `@Transactional` (a new transaction per call), and is invoked once per housemate plus once for the new owner.

### Business-rule / side-effect helpers invoked (all in `rest/controller/v1/`)

- `BusinessDays.effective(owner)` — `BusinessDays.java:19-25`. Defaults registration date to `LocalDate.now()` when null, and rolls a weekend forward to Monday. Result is stored on the owner (controller line 105).
- Daily-cap check (controller lines 106-109): if ≥100 existing owners already share the effective registration date, returns `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`.
- `Identities.isDuplicate(owners, owner)` — `Identities.java:20-29`. Builds an identity key `normalizedTelephone|email|householdId` and returns `409 CONFLICT` on a whole-key match.
- `CityCodes.countInCity(owners, owner)` — `CityCodes.java:17-19`. Case-insensitive count of owners in the same city; ≥50 returns `409 CONFLICT` (controller line 113).
- `Households.joinHousehold(owners, owner, sharesHousehold)` — `Households.java:37-49`. Side-effecting: only when `sharesHousehold == true`; if housemates exist (same normalized lastName+address), it stamps a stable `householdId` (UUID derived from the household key) on the candidate and every housemate, sets `householdMemberCount`, and returns the housemates. The controller then persists each returned housemate via `saveOwner` (controller lines 116-117) — a mutation/side-effect on OTHER existing rows.
- `owner.assignCustomerCode(CityCodes.countInCity(...))` (controller line 118) — see entity section; generates `customerCode`.
- `Namesakes.count(owners, owner)` — `Namesakes.java:17-19`. Case-insensitive count of same first+last name owners; stored via `setNamesakeCount`.
- `BulkSignups.exceededDailyLimit(owners, owner)` — `BulkSignups.java:17-21`. Flags `true` when >80 existing owners share the registration date; stored via `setBulkSignupWarning`.

Note two helper methods exist but are NOT called by `addOwner`: `Emails.isDuplicate(...)` (`Emails.java:18-24`, email-uniqueness) and `Households.isDuplicate(...)` (`Households.java:25-30`). Email uniqueness is therefore not enforced on create despite the helper existing.

---

## 5. Repository methods

Interface: `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java` declares `findAll()` (line 74) and `save(Owner)` (line 66).

Active implementation (profile `spring-data-jpa`): `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java`, line 37 — `extends OwnerRepository, Repository<Owner, Integer>`. `save` and `findAll()` (no-arg) are not overridden here, so Spring Data provides the standard implementations (`save` = persist/merge by id; `findAll` = select all). The `@Query`-annotated methods (lines 39-57) are for the by-lastName / paged / by-id variants, not used by create.

Alternate implementation (profile `jpa`, inactive here): `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, `save` at lines 92-100 does `em.persist(owner)` when id is null else `em.merge(owner)`; `findAll()` at lines 102-107. This class is annotated `@Repository` (line 44), which is what the AOP aspect in section 8 joins on.

No `existsBy` methods exist; uniqueness is done in-memory in the controller helpers.

---

## 6. Owner entity lifecycle callbacks / auditing

`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`
- Class annotations, lines 34-37: `@Entity`, `@Table(name = "owners")`, `@EntityListeners(OwnerAuditListener.class)`.
- `@PrePersist` callback, lines 78-83:
```java
@PrePersist
private void defaultRegistrationDate() {
    if (this.registrationDate == null) {
        this.registrationDate = java.time.LocalDate.now();
    }
}
```
(This is a fallback; the controller already sets the date via `BusinessDays.effective`.)
- `assignCustomerCode(int)`, lines 112-116: builds `customerCode` as `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (zero-padded sequence = existing city count + 1).
- Setters normalize/mutate input state:
  - `setEmail` (lines 154-156): lower-cases the email.
  - `setAddress` (lines 162-164): runs `PostalAddress.normalize(...)`.
  - `setTelephone` (lines 178-180): runs `TelephoneNumber.toE164(...)`.
- Entity-level Bean Validation: `address`, `city`, `telephone` are `@NotEmpty` (lines 39, 43, 47) and `telephone` also `@Pattern(regexp = "^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$", ...)` (line 48). `Person` (superclass) adds `@NotEmpty` on `firstName`/`lastName` (`Person.java:32,36`). These fire only if an entity-level validation pass runs (e.g. Hibernate `pre-persist` validation), not from the controller's `@Valid` on the DTO.

The normalizers:
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/TelephoneNumber.java` (lines 32-45): strips non-digits, keeps `+CC` when input starts with `+`, else assumes `+61` and drops a leading `0`.
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/PostalAddress.java` (lines 34-47): trims/collapses whitespace, upper-cases, expands `ST→STREET`, `RD→ROAD`, `AVE→AVENUE`.

