## 4. Service-layer method(s)

The controller calls two `ClinicService` methods during creation: `findAllOwners()` and `saveOwner(...)`.

`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`
- `findAllOwners()`, lines 125-129:
```java
@Override
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public Collection<Owner> findAllOwners() throws DataAccessException {
    return ownerRepository.findAll();
}
```
- `saveOwner(Owner)`, lines 232-237:
```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```
The service is a thin facade; it has no validation, uniqueness, or business logic of its own. All the business rules run in the controller via the helper classes below. `saveOwner` is `@Transactional` (a new transaction per call), and is invoked once per housemate plus once for the new owner.

### Business-rule / side-effect helpers invoked (all in `rest/controller/v1/`)

- `BusinessDays.effective(owner)` — `BusinessDays.java:19-25`. Defaults registration date to `LocalDate.now()` when null, and rolls a weekend forward to Monday. Result is stored on the owner (controller line 105).
- Daily-cap check (controller lines 106-109): if ≥100 existing owners already share the effective registration date, returns `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`.
- `Identities.isDuplicate(owners, owner)` — `Identities.java:20-29`. Builds an identity key `normalizedTelephone|email|householdId` and returns `409 CONFLICT` on a whole-key match.
- `CityCodes.countInCity(owners, owner)` — `CityCodes.java:17-19`. Case-insensitive count of owners in the same city; ≥50 returns `409 CONFLICT` (controller line 113).
- `Households.joinHousehold(owners, owner, sharesHousehold)` — `Households.java:37-49`. Side-effecting: only when `sharesHousehold == true`; if housemates exist (same normalized lastName+address), it stamps a stable `householdId` (UUID derived from the household key) on the candidate and every housemate, sets `householdMemberCount`, and returns the housemates. The controller then persists each returned housemate via `saveOwner` (controller lines 116-117) — a mutation/side-effect on OTHER existing rows.
- `owner.assignCustomerCode(CityCodes.countInCity(...))` (controller line 118) — see entity section; generates `customerCode`.
- `Namesakes.count(owners, owner)` — `Namesakes.java:17-19`. Case-insensitive count of same first+last name owners; stored via `setNamesakeCount`.
- `BulkSignups.exceededDailyLimit(owners, owner)` — `BulkSignups.java:17-21`. Flags `true` when >80 existing owners share the registration date; stored via `setBulkSignupWarning`.

Note two helper methods exist but are NOT called by `addOwner`: `Emails.isDuplicate(...)` (`Emails.java:18-24`, email-uniqueness) and `Households.isDuplicate(...)` (`Households.java:25-30`). Email uniqueness is therefore not enforced on create despite the helper existing.

